1. What is a pointer, and how did you use it in your program? (3 pts)
A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable rather than storing a value directly.
In the program, Patient* p is a pointer to a Patient struct. Instead of holding patient data itself, p holds the address of patient1.
This allows indirect access to patient1's data through p.

2. What does the & operator do in your program? (3 pts)
The & (address-of) operator returns the memory address of a variable.
In the line Patient* p = &patient1;, the & operator retrieves the memory address where patient1 is stored and assigns that address to the pointer p.
Without &, p would have no valid address to point to.

